-                                                                        <p  class="paragraph mt-0">Above the roofs of Podil¹, a brick tower rises, <a rel="nofollow" href="https://diaz.kyiv.ua/objects/provulok-horyva-1-kalancha-vogneborczi-ta-muzejna/">built in 1910</a> for a former fire department: firefighters on duty were on lookout here to spot fires in the city. In the 1980s, firefighters from the Kyiv Regional Fire Department worked here. After the disaster at the Chornobyl²&nbsp;Nuclear Power Plant (CNPP) on April 26, 1986, representatives from the department <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.istpravda.com.ua/short/2026/05/24/165904/">participated</a> in extinguishing the fire at the destroyed reactor #4.&nbsp;</p>
+                                                                        <p  class="paragraph mt-0">Above the roofs of Podil<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-1" id="fnref-1" aria-label="Footnote 1">¹</a></sup>, a brick tower rises, <a rel="nofollow" href="https://diaz.kyiv.ua/objects/provulok-horyva-1-kalancha-vogneborczi-ta-muzejna/">built in 1910</a> for a former fire department: firefighters on duty were on lookout here to spot fires in the city. In the 1980s, firefighters from the Kyiv Regional Fire Department worked here. After the disaster at the Chornobyl<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-2" id="fnref-2" aria-label="Footnote 2">²</a></sup>&nbsp;Nuclear Power Plant (CNPP) on April 26, 1986, representatives from the department <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.istpravda.com.ua/short/2026/05/24/165904/">participated</a> in extinguishing the fire at the destroyed reactor #4.&nbsp;</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">Six years later, the Chornobyl Museum opened in this yellow brick building; the firefighters <a rel="nofollow" href="https://chornobyl-museum.kyiv.ua/about#history">collected the first exhibits</a>. They launched a photo documentary exhibition dedicated to the anniversary of the Chornobyl tragedy; it later became a permanent display commemorating the country’s largest man-made disaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
-                                                    <p  class="paragraph">The museum’s funds were accumulated over the years, and it eventually became one of the world's largest archives on the Chornobyl disaster. Here, documents from government commissions, technical materials from the CNPP, personal belongings of the response team, and household items from the Chornobyl Polissia³&nbsp;region are on display.</p>
+                                                    <p  class="paragraph">The museum’s funds were accumulated over the years, and it eventually became one of the world's largest archives on the Chornobyl disaster. Here, documents from government commissions, technical materials from the CNPP, personal belongings of the response team, and household items from the Chornobyl Polissia<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-3" id="fnref-3" aria-label="Footnote 3">³</a></sup>&nbsp;region are on display.</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">On May 24, at night, almost all of these items were buried by fire, water, a layer of dust, and glass shards. During a massive air raid on the capital, a <a rel="nofollow" href="https://life.pravda.com.ua/culture/muzeji-cerkvi-vish-ta-teatr-shcho-vidomo-pro-naslidki-ataki-po-kiyevu-315148/">Russian missile destroyed</a> the building of the Chornobyl National Museum. It <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mvs.gov.ua/news/za-lastunkami-nacionalnogo-muzeiu-cornobil-cogo-ne-vidno-v-ekspoziciyi-ta-pro-shho-govoriat-vidverto">opened after a renovation</a> only one month before the attack. The museum employees managed to evacuate most of the exhibits in the morning after the strike, but some artifacts were lost for good, and the building is severely damaged.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">Under the tall fire tower, only fragments of walls and floors remain. Light-yellow brick is torn with cracks. A line of broken pavement runs along the facade, and in the courtyard, the ground is covered with piles of broken bricks, plaster fragments, and metal fragments.</p>
