+                                                        <p  class="paragraph">Museum employees compare the reforms of Emskyi-Mohylevskyi to the work of Oleksandr Roitburd<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-4" id="fnref-4" aria-label="Footnote 4">⁴</a></sup> in the same position. There’s a distance of almost a century between them, but here, in this display, their paths cross.</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">Emskyi-Mohylevskyi actively expanded the collection; he traveled to Moscow and Saint Petersburg and literally fought to acquire artworks for the museum. Roitburd also took every opportunity to bring contemporary Ukrainian art and new projects to the museum.</p>
-                                                    <p  class="paragraph">Both Emskyi-Mohylevskyi and Roitburd were working during a shift in the societal paradigm. For the former, it was the early Soviet Union, for the latter, Ukraine after the Revolution of Dignity⁵, which was reconsidering its own identity and the role of culture in society. In both cases, the museum became a battlefield in shaping the city's cultural memory.</p>
+                                                    <p  class="paragraph">Both Emskyi-Mohylevskyi and Roitburd were working during a shift in the societal paradigm. For the former, it was the early Soviet Union, for the latter, Ukraine after the Revolution of Dignity<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-5" id="fnref-5" aria-label="Footnote 5">⁵</a></sup>, which was reconsidering its own identity and the role of culture in society. In both cases, the museum became a battlefield in shaping the city's cultural memory.</p>
                                                     <figure class="image">
             <picture class="image__picture">
                 <img src="../storage/posts/attaches/5cfb7ccdf4add826aeefaa92b1567e82153c3ccb.webp" alt="Remembering the Oblivion. How the Odesa National Fine Arts Museum rescued its collection from the Russian attack №12" title="Remembering the Oblivion. How the Odesa National Fine Arts Museum rescued its collection from the Russian attack №12">
@@ -260,7 +260,7 @@ Arkhyp Kuindzhi, and Mykola Pymonenko.
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">The attack happened on November 5, 2023, on the museum’s birthday. “There was a big hole along the street; the fence was bent. Today, the museum looks much better. It was a huge amount of work: collecting all the fragments, putting them into separate boxes, and labeling everything with what came from where. And then, the stabilization.” But the museum fully reopened on February 15 and hasn’t closed its doors since.&nbsp;</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">“Currently, our windows are still barred; there’s barbed wire in some places. A lot of people even think that we are closed. But in fact, we are waiting for everyone to visit,” Kateryna says.&nbsp;</p>
                                                     <h3 class="heading heading--h3">Footnotes</h3>
-                                                            <ol class="footnotes"><li value="1">The first massive Russian air raid started around 4 a.m. February 24, 2022, initiating the full-scale invasion. Many Ukrainian cities were targeted with missile strikes at once.</li><li value="2">In Soviet times, the "general party line" was a phrase used to describe the current ideological direction of the ruling Communist Party in the USSR at any given time.</li><li value="3">In Soviet times, there were so-called special funds in libraries, museums, and other institutions where books and artworks that didn't fit the Party's ideology were stored. Access to these funds was severely restricted.</li><li value="4">Oleksandr Roitburd is one of the most prominent artists from Odesa who was active in independent Ukraine. He was the director of the Museum from 2018 to his death in 2021.</li><li value="5">In 2013-2014, there were massive ongoing protests against the policies of the pro-Russian president Viktor Yanukovych and his government that lasted for many months. Yanukovych fled to Russia as a result, and the event became known as the Revolution of Dignity. As Yanukovych fled, Russia annexed Crimea and started the war in Donbas, and it was a time of big shifts in the collective mind of Ukrainians.</li></ol>
