+                                                        <p  class="paragraph">At first, they visited Stefan Banach’s grave at the Lychakiv Cemetery<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-2" id="fnref-2" aria-label="Footnote 2">²</a></sup>, later Myron Zarytskyi’s; then they began to discover others. This is how the idea emerged to create a special map of burials of Lviv mathematicians, gather their biographies on a <a rel="nofollow" href="http://www.math.lviv.ua/mathmemorial/uk/index.php">website</a>, and then place QR codes next to each grave. Now, anyone can read the biography of a person buried here and discover the whole tour about the Lviv School of Mathematics while visiting the cemetery.</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">The first to appreciate this weren’t even tourists, but relatives of the mathematicians. For instance, after they placed a QR code at Myron Zarytskyi’s grave, his great-granddaughter, Ustia Soroka, texted the researchers to say that her children were excited to scan the code and read about their great-great-grandpa. Now, Iryna and Olena regularly return to the Lychakiv Cemetery not only as historians of science but also as custodians of their mathematical map of Lviv.</p>
                                                     <h3 class="heading heading--h3">The grave of Józefa Sabatowska, mother of Waleria Sabatowska</h3>
                                                             <p  class="paragraph">This burial is the most recent discovery by the researchers, so instead of the usual route that should start with Banach’s grave, we visit it first to see the discovery. Here, in 1945, Józefa Sabatowska, mother of mathematician Waleria Sabatowska, was buried. Like many educated women of those times, Waleria didn’t have opportunities for an academic career. She became a mathematics teacher instead; teaching was virtually the only way for women to remain in a scientific context.</p>
