                                                     <h3 class="heading heading--h3">From mathematicians to philosophers: the grave of Kazimierz Twardowski</h3>
                                                             <p  class="paragraph">The researchers don’t rush their tours. A tour may last up to three hours, sometimes even longer. “Once, we were leading a tour in pouring rain. Only one participant stayed to the end; she wasn’t a mathematician, just very interested. There’s a lot to tell indeed,” Iryna shares.</p>
-                                                    <p  class="paragraph">Not all representatives of the Lviv School of Mathematics have graves that one can show to visitors. During the tour, the researchers mention three professors — Antoni Łomnicki, Włodzimierz Stożek, and Stanisław Ruziewicz — who died during the Massacre of Lviv Professors. On the night of July 3–4, 1941, the Nazis murdered over forty Lviv scientists and their family members on the Vulka Hills⁶. Some mathematicians of the Lviv School of Mathematics were among them. Today, a memorial monument commemorates the location of the executions, but there are no separate burials that could be included in the tour. But still, the researchers tell visitors about them, because the history of mathematics in Lviv wouldn’t be full without this tragic page.</p>
+                                                    <p  class="paragraph">Not all representatives of the Lviv School of Mathematics have graves that one can show to visitors. During the tour, the researchers mention three professors — Antoni Łomnicki, Włodzimierz Stożek, and Stanisław Ruziewicz — who died during the Massacre of Lviv Professors. On the night of July 3–4, 1941, the Nazis murdered over forty Lviv scientists and their family members on the Vulka Hills<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-6" id="fnref-6" aria-label="Footnote 6">⁶</a></sup>. Some mathematicians of the Lviv School of Mathematics were among them. Today, a memorial monument commemorates the location of the executions, but there are no separate burials that could be included in the tour. But still, the researchers tell visitors about them, because the history of mathematics in Lviv wouldn’t be full without this tragic page.</p>
                                                     <p  class="paragraph">The next stop on the tour can be unexpected — after mathematicians, the tour leads to the grave of the philosopher Kazimierz Twardowski. But, as we’ve already explained, future mathematicians, physicists, and chemists were studying at the Faculty of Philosophy, where all the students had to study philosophy; so the history of the Lviv School of Mathematics is tightly connected to the history of the Lviv-Warsaw School — one of the most influential intellectual currents in Central-Eastern Europe, founded by Kazimierz Twardowski.</p>
