                                                         <p  class="paragraph">Twardowski moved to Lviv from Vienna at the end of the 19th century and quickly became one of the key figures in the university’s life. Besides holding lectures on philosophy, logic, and psychology, he had a much broader goal — to change the educational culture itself. He was of the opinion that philosophy shouldn’t be a discipline restricted to future scientists. He advocated teaching the basics of philosophical thinking in schools. For this reason, he was actively developing a course titled “Propedeutics of Philosophy,” an introduction of sorts on how to think, pose questions, and present arguments in favor of your position.</p>
-                                                    <p  class="paragraph">His role in opening university education for women was no less important. Twardowski became one of the most persistent advocates of women’s right to study at a university. At his initiative, special preparatory courses for women were organized, enabling them to receive the required education before universities officially opened to women. The researchers tell us how, even recently, they’ve found in the archives the lists of attendees for these courses and the subjects they were studying. Twardowski also supported the development of women's school education, since university admission was impossible without passing the matura⁷.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
+                                                    <p  class="paragraph">His role in opening university education for women was no less important. Twardowski became one of the most persistent advocates of women’s right to study at a university. At his initiative, special preparatory courses for women were organized, enabling them to receive the required education before universities officially opened to women. The researchers tell us how, even recently, they’ve found in the archives the lists of attendees for these courses and the subjects they were studying. Twardowski also supported the development of women's school education, since university admission was impossible without passing the matura<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn-7" id="fnref-7" aria-label="Footnote 7">⁷</a></sup>.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
